{
  "concept": "An element's group position reliably predicts its common ionic charge for main-group elements (cations equal to the group number, anions equal to the steps left of the noble gases), but transition metals break this rule by forming several variable charges.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "title 'Periodic Table of the Elements' centered at top",
    "group-number labels 1 through 18 in blue across the top edge",
    "period-number labels 1 through 7 in purple down the left edge",
    "Group 1 alkali-metal cells (Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr) shaded purple, each labeled with a +1 charge (Li+, Na+, K+, Rb+, Cs+, Fr+)",
    "Group 2 alkaline-earth cells (Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba, Ra) shaded green, each labeled +2 (Be2+, Mg2+, Ca2+, Sr2+, Ba2+, Ra2+)",
    "Group 1 hydrogen cell (period 1) shaded pale gray, no charge label",
    "d-block transition-metal cells (groups 3-12) shaded tan/cream, labeled with charges including variable pairs Cr3+/Cr6+, Fe2+/Fe3+, Cu+/Cu2+, Au+/Au3+, Hg2 2+/Hg2+ and singles Mn2+, Co2+, Ni2+, Zn2+, Ag+, Cd2+, Pt2+",
    "Group 13 cell Al3+ (white background)",
    "Group 14 cell C4- (white background)",
    "Group 15 pnictogen cells (N3-, P3-, As3-) shaded pink",
    "Group 16 chalcogen cells (O2-, S2-, Se2-, Te2-) shaded pink",
    "Group 17 halogen cells (F-, Cl-, Br-, I-, At-) shaded lavender",
    "Group 18 noble-gas cells (He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe, Rn) shaded cyan with no charge label",
    "remaining empty white cells (B, Si, and unmarked p-block/metalloid positions)",
    "red outline box around the group-3 cells of periods 6 and 7",
    "curved red arrow from the red box down to the two detached f-block rows",
    "lanthanide row (*) shaded green and actinide row (**) shaded pink, drawn as two detached strips below the main table"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "groupSweep",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Group number",
      "range": [1, 18, 1],
      "default": 1,
      "teaches": "Sweeping the groups left to right makes the systematic trend visible: groups 1-2 form cations whose positive charge equals the group number, groups 15-17 form anions whose negative charge equals 18 minus the group number, and the central d-block refuses a single value"
    },
    {
      "id": "elementCell",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Selecting one cell reveals that element's specific ion charge, its electron configuration, and the group-trend rule that explains the charge — showing the charge is just the count of electrons lost or gained to reach a stable (often noble-gas) configuration"
    },
    {
      "id": "colorScheme",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Color scheme",
      "options": ["Element type", "Ion charge"],
      "default": "Element type",
      "teaches": "Comparing element-type coloring with charge-value coloring shows that the charge pattern is not random: it tracks the metal/nonmetal/noble-gas classification, and recoloring by charge magnitude makes the smooth +2 -> 0 -> -3 sweep across a period pop out"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Cations on the left",
      "narration": "With element-type coloring and the slider on group 1, the highlighted column shows every alkali metal as a +1 cation; group 2 is +2. The positive charge simply equals the group number because these metals lose their outer electrons.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 1, "colorScheme": "Element type" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Anions on the right",
      "narration": "Sweep the slider to group 17: the halogens are all -1, group 16 is -2, group 15 is -3. The negative charge equals 18 minus the group number — the electrons needed to complete the noble-gas shell.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 17, "colorScheme": "Element type" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Transition metals break the rule",
      "narration": "Move the slider into the d-block (e.g. group 8) and click iron: it shows Fe2+ AND Fe3+, with no single group-number rule. Click chromium or copper to see the same variability — position alone cannot predict transition-metal charge.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 8, "colorScheme": "Element type" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two ways to see the same pattern",
      "narration": "Toggle to ion-charge coloring: the table recolors by charge magnitude, turning the metal-to-nonmetal transition into a smooth visual gradient and making the noble-gas column (charge 0) read as the neutral pivot of the whole trend.",
      "state": { "groupSweep": 1, "colorScheme": "Ion charge" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "For main-group elements the common ionic charge is read straight off the group number (lose that many electrons, or gain up to the noble-gas configuration), but transition metals form multiple variable charges that no group-position rule can predict.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Lay out a standard 18-column periodic grid with group numbers 1-18 in blue along the top and period numbers 1-7 in purple down the left. Color families exactly as in the source: group 1 purple, group 2 green, d-block tan/cream, groups 15-16 pink, group 17 lavender, group 18 cyan, remaining p-block cells white. Write each element's common ion charge inside its cell, stacking two charges (e.g. Cr3+/Cr6+, Fe2+/Fe3+, Cu+/Cu2+, Au+/Au3+, Hg2 2+/Hg2+) where the element is variable. Leave noble gases and the period-1 hydrogen cell without a charge. Draw a red rectangle around the group-3 cells of periods 6-7 with a curved red arrow pointing to two detached strips below (green lanthanides *, pink actinides **). For the slider, outline or brighten the active column; for click, draw a bold highlight ring on the chosen cell plus a small info card with charge, electron configuration, and the governing rule; for the toggle, swap the per-cell fill from the element-type palette to a charge-magnitude palette (deep warm for high positive, neutral gray for 0, deep cool for high negative)."
}

