      "options": [
        "Element type",
        "Ion charge"
      ],
      "default": "Element type",
      "teaches": "Comparing element-type coloring with charge-value coloring shows that the charge pattern is not random: it tracks the metal/nonmetal/noble-gas classification, and recoloring by charge magnitude makes the smooth +2 -> 0 -> -3 sweep across a period pop out"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Cations on the left",
      "narration": "With element-type coloring and the slider on group 1, the highlighted column shows every alkali metal as a +1 cation; group 2 is +2. The positive charge simply equals the group number because these metals lose their outer electrons.",
      "state": {
        "groupSweep": 1,
        "colorScheme": "Element type"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Anions on the right",
      "narration": "Sweep the slider to group 17: the halogens are all -1, group 16 is -2, group 15 is -3. The negative charge equals 18 minus the group number — the electrons needed to complete the noble-gas shell.",
