      "state": {
        "groupSweep": 8,
        "colorScheme": "Element type"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two ways to see the same pattern",
      "narration": "Toggle to ion-charge coloring: the table recolors by charge magnitude, turning the metal-to-nonmetal transition into a smooth visual gradient and making the noble-gas column (charge 0) read as the neutral pivot of the whole trend.",
      "state": {
        "groupSweep": 1,
        "colorScheme": "Ion charge"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "For main-group elements the common ionic charge is read straight off the group number (lose that many electrons, or gain up to the noble-gas configuration), but transition metals form multiple variable charges that no group-position rule can predict.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Lay out a standard 18-column periodic grid with group numbers 1-18 in blue along the top and period numbers 1-7 in purple down the left. Color families exactly as in the source: group 1 purple, group 2 green, d-block tan/cream, groups 15-16 pink, group 17 lavender, group 18 cyan, remaining p-block cells white. Write each element's common ion charge inside its cell, stacking two charges (e.g. Cr3+/Cr6+, Fe2+/Fe3+, Cu+/Cu2+, Au+/Au3+, Hg2 2+/Hg2+) where the element is variable. Leave noble gases and the period-1 hydrogen cell without a charge. Draw a red rectangle around the group-3 cells of periods 6-7 with a curved red arrow pointing to two detached strips below (green lanthanides *, pink actinides **). For the slider, outline or brighten the active column; for click, draw a bold highlight ring on the chosen cell plus a small info card with charge, electron configuration, and the governing rule; for the toggle, swap the per-cell fill from the element-type palette to a charge-magnitude palette (deep warm for high positive, neutral gray for 0, deep cool for high negative)."
}

